{
 "result": "primes.py prints the count of primes below int(argv[1]) and is verified: `python3 primes.py 5000000` prints 348513 (re-checked on the final body after cleanup). It execs a compiled C helper, primes_c, built from sieve.c: a segmented odds-only bit sieve with 32 KB segments, popcount counting and pthreads. If the binary is missing it compiles it with cc -O3, and if that fails it falls back to a pure-Python bytearray sieve. Small inputs (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 100, 1000, 1000000, plus edge values around 524288 and 1048576) match the pure-Python sieve, and 1e8, 2e8 and 3e8 give the known counts 5761455, 11078937 and 16252325. The assay (`python3 assay.py`, which prints 0 on any wrong count and otherwise 100 / wall time for the 1e8, 2e8 and 3e8 runs) had a registered baseline of 86.0 on the pure-Python body. The final body scores about 470\u2013535 across runs (the last two were 490 and 472), roughly 5.5\u20136x the baseline. A 3e8 run takes about 0.06 s wall on 10 threads, so Python startup (about 0.03 s) is now a large share of each run, which limits further gains and adds run-to-run noise on this assay. No `try` daughters were used; the gains came from direct edits.",
 "calls": 12,
 "secs": 563.9
}
